    title: "Reading Passage",
    level: "C1",
    text: "The construction industry transforms architectural designs into physical reality — a complex process involving dozens of trades, materials, and regulatory requirements. Every project begins with groundworks: excavating foundations, laying drainage, and preparing the site. Foundations — typically concrete poured into trenches — anchor the building to the ground and distribute its weight. For the structural frame, options include traditional masonry (brick and block), timber frame, steel frame, or reinforced concrete. Bricklaying remains central to British construction — the red brick terraced house is an enduring symbol of the building tradition. Roofing follows the frame: timber trusses support tiles (clay, concrete, or slate), with felt and battens providing weatherproofing. First fix — the initial installation of services hidden within walls and floors — includes plumbing (water pipes and waste), electrical wiring, and heating pipework. Plastering smooths internal walls, creating a surface ready for decoration. Second fix completes the services: fitting sockets, switches, radiators, sanitaryware (basins, toilets, baths), and kitchen units. Joinery — doors, skirting boards, architraves, and window frames — adds the finishing carpentry. External works include landscaping, driveways, fencing, and drainage. Throughout, building control inspectors visit at key stages to ensure compliance with Building Regulations — the legal standards covering structural safety, fire protection, insulation, ventilation, and accessibility. A project manager coordinates the entire process, ensuring trades arrive in the correct sequence and the build stays on budget and schedule.",
